.\" zvolrescue(1) — manual page, mdoc. Kept true to the program by
.\" tests/man-check.sh, which reads --help and this file on every push.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ZVOLRESCUE 1
.Os zvolrescue 0.9.8
.Sh NAME
.Nm zvolrescue
.Nd read-only extraction of zvols from ZFS pools that no longer import
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm scan
.Op Ar options
.Ar dev ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm list
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm dump
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar out.img
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the on-disk format of a ZFS pool by itself, without the kernel's
ZFS and without importing the pool, and writes the raw image of a
volume (a zvol) out of it, at any transaction group (TXG) that still
verifies.
It reads its inputs and never writes to them: labels, uberblocks and
every block are read through their own checksums, mirror copies and
RAIDZ or dRAID parity are used to heal what one member cannot give,
and an output that would land on an input device is refused.
.Pp
The pool's members are given as devices, partitions or image files.
Every command takes the same
.Ar global options ,
and
.Cm list
and
.Cm dump
take the same
.Sx POOL MEMBER OPTIONS .
A JSON Lines evidence log of every run, with the hash of every output,
is written with
.Fl -evidence-log ;
.Xr zvolreport 1
turns those logs into one checkable report.
.Sh GLOBAL OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -format Ar text | json
Output format: human-readable text (the default), or one JSON document
on standard output.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Increase verbosity; repeatable.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Suppress non-essential output.
.It Fl -evidence-log Ar file
Append a JSON Lines evidence log to
.Ar file :
what was opened, what was read, what was written and its hash.
.It Fl -hash-inputs
Also record the SHA-256 of every input in the evidence log.
Off by default: hashing a shelf of disk images means reading all of
them through, which can take hours.
Outputs are always hashed.
.It Fl -no-color
Disable colour in text output.
.It Fl -debug
Trace every read decision (labels, uberblocks, blocks, dnodes, ZAPs,
the DSL walk) with hex dumps on failures, to standard error.
.It Fl -debug-log Ar file
Write the
.Fl -debug
trace to
.Ar file
instead of standard error; implies
.Fl -debug .
.El
.Sh POOL MEMBER OPTIONS
How the pool is put together from what was given.
Taken by
.Cm list
and
.Cm dump ;
.Cm scan
takes those of them it names.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -image Ar file
An image file that is a member of the pool; repeatable.
Devices are given as plain arguments.
.It Fl -hints Ar file
Describe the layout by hand when the labels cannot: a JSON file with
the ashift, the top-level vdevs and their members in vdev order.
It is used exactly as a label would be; every block read through it
is still verified by its checksum.
.It Fl -search-order
Try every member order the layout leaves open and keep the one the
checksums accept.
Only for raidz and draid, where order is what a DVA addresses.
.It Fl -pool-guid Ar guid
Select a pool by GUID when several are found on the members.
.It Fl -assume-member Ar path Ns Op = Ns Ar guid
Treat a member whose labels are gone as a leaf the configuration says
is missing:
.Ar path
when only one leaf is missing, or
.Ar path Ns = Ns Ar guid
to name it.
Repeatable.
Nothing is taken on trust: every block read through it is still
verified by its checksum.
.It Fl -ignore-unknown-features
Read a pool whose active feature flags this build cannot account for.
Without this the run refuses and names them.
With it the pool is read as though the features were not there, which
can be wrong in a way no checksum objects to: a reflowed raidz hands
back the checksums of other blocks, and they agree.
.It Fl -map Ar member Ns = Ns Ar mapfile
The GNU ddrescue mapfile an image was made with; repeatable.
Sectors the imager could not read are refused before they are read, so
a block that crosses them is kept as far as the imager did read it and
the rest is zeros with the reason, not a checksum failure with none.
.It Fl -device-may-fail
Go on after a device refuses a read.
Without this the first refused read on a device stops the run with
exit status 7, and the disk is for an imager.
With it the refused range is skipped once, zeros, the reason logged,
nothing read twice, and the run stops anyway after 8 incidents.
For the operator who has weighed it and needs one small object off a
disk that cannot be imaged.
.It Fl -surface-scan-on-device
Search the surface of a block device: for uberblock anchors when its
labels are gone, for a root when no uberblock survives.
Refused without this, because a surface scan is what a disk with
defects survives least: image the disk first, and scan the image.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss scan
.Nm Cm scan
.Op Ar options
.Ar dev ...
.Pp
What is on these devices or images: labels, uberblocks, the TXG
window, which pool each member belongs to and where in it, what is
missing, what is stale.
.Fl v
adds the uberblock ring of every label, corrupt slots included.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -zero-point
Search for the vdev's zero point (where its label area begins) even
when its labels are readable.
The search runs by itself on a member whose four label configurations
are all unusable.
.It Fl -zero-point-whole
Search the whole member for the zero point, not just its first and
last 64 MiB.
.It Fl -psize Ar bytes
Physical size to assume for the vdev when testing the rear label pair;
repeatable.
The front pair needs no hypothesis.
.It Fl -hints Ar file
Describe the layout by hand: the JSON template that
.Fl -emit-label
turns into a label.
.It Fl -emit-label Ar file
Write the label the layout describes for one member to
.Ar file :
a 256 KiB image with the configuration nvlist sealed for its label
position, to place on a
.Em copy
of the disk.
The geometry goes to
.Ar file Ns .json
beside it.
Nothing is ever written to the evidence.
.It Fl -emit-for Ar top : Ns Ar leaf
Which member of the layout the emitted label belongs to; default
.Ql 0:0 .
.It Fl -emit-label-index Ar n
Which of the four label positions to seal the emitted label for;
default 0.
A label verifies only at the offset it was sealed for.
.It Fl -map , Fl -device-may-fail , Fl -surface-scan-on-device
As under
.Sx POOL MEMBER OPTIONS .
.El
.Ss list
.Nm Cm list
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Pp
List the datasets, volumes and snapshots of the pool at one TXG, with
type, GUID, creation TXG and time, referenced size, and for volumes
their size and block size.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -txg Ar n
Transaction group to read; default the newest that verifies.
.It Fl -before Ar timestamp
The newest TXG synced at or before this time, RFC 3339 or Unix
seconds.
.It Fl -diff Ar txg2
Also show what was created or destroyed relative to this TXG, with the
.Fl -txg
that gets each destroyed dataset back.
.It Fl r , Fl -recursive
Also list snapshots.
.It Fl p , Fl -properties
Show the properties each dataset has set, user properties included.
Only what was set on the dataset itself is on disk; anything else is
inherited or a default.
.El
.Ss dump
.Nm Cm dump
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar out.img
.Op Ar options
.Pp
Extract a volume to a raw sparse image, verifying every block by its
checksum, healing from another copy or from parity where one member
fails, and hashing the image as it is written.
The dataset comes first so that the variable-length list of members
can follow it.
Without
.Fl -txg
the TXG is the newest verified one that names the dataset and still
reads its object set; one whose object set has been reused since a
destroy is passed over for an older one, and the record says so.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar out.img
The output image; a directory with
.Fl r .
Refused if it resolves onto an input device.
.It Fl -txg Ar n
Transaction group to read.
.It Fl -strict
Abort on the first unreadable block instead of writing zeros for it.
.It Fl -key Ar keyspec
Encryption key of an encrypted dataset:
.Ql raw: Ns Ar file ,
.Ql hex: Ns Ar hex ,
.Ql passphrase: Ns Ar file ,
or
.Ql prompt .
.It Fl -resume
Continue an interrupted extraction of the same dataset and TXG.
A SIGINT or SIGTERM ends a run at the next block with its state
written (exit status 6); the state is kept in
.Pa out.img.resume.json
while a run is in progress, and a second signal kills the run the
usual way.
.It Fl r , Fl -recursive
Extract every volume under
.Ar dataset
into the directory
.Ar out ,
one image per volume plus
.Pa manifest.json .
.It Fl -hash Ar list
Also hash the image with these, as it is written, for a toolchain
that wants them:
.Ql md5 ,
.Ql sha1 ,
comma-separated.
SHA-256 is always taken.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa out.img.resume.json
The state of an interrupted
.Cm dump ,
beside its output; removed when the image is whole.
.It Pa manifest.json
Under the output directory of
.Cm dump Fl r :
every image written, its dataset, TXG, size and hash.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
Usage error, including a missing or wrong encryption key.
.It 2
Evidence unreadable: a member could not be opened or read.
.It 3
The pool is unrecoverable at the requested TXG, or the dataset is not
readable at any TXG.
.It 4
The image is not the whole volume: blocks that could not be read were
written as zeros and counted, or
.Fl -strict
aborted at the first one.
.It 5
Refused: the output would land on an input, or a device would have
been surface-scanned without leave.
.It 6
Interrupted by a signal; the state is written and
.Fl -resume
continues.
.It 7
The medium refused a read: a device, not an image, returned an I/O
error and the run stopped there.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
What the two halves of a mirror hold:
.Dl zvolrescue scan /dev/ada0p3 /dev/ada1p3
.Pp
The datasets at the newest TXG, and what was destroyed since TXG
4816229:
.Dl zvolrescue list -r --diff 4816229 /dev/ada0p3 /dev/ada1p3
.Pp
The destroyed volume back, from the TXG before the destroy, with an
evidence log:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zvolrescue --evidence-log case42.jsonl dump pool/vm/disk0 \e
    /dev/ada0p3 /dev/ada1p3 --txg 4816229 -o /mnt/rescue/disk0.img
.Ed
.Pp
The same from images made with ddrescue, with their mapfiles so that
what the imager could not read is refused by name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zvolrescue dump pool/vm/disk0 --image ada0.img --image ada1.img \e
    --map ada0.img=ada0.map --map ada1.img=ada1.map -o disk0.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zvolcarve 1 ,
.Xr zvolfiles 1 ,
.Xr zvolreport 1 ,
.Xr zvoltimeline 1 ,
.Xr zdb 8 ,
.Xr zpool 8
.Pp
The specification, the companion tools and the real-world test matrix
are in the
.Pa docs
directory of the source and at
.Lk https://github.com/click0/zvolrescue .
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
recovers volumes, not pools: it never writes to a pool and cannot make
one import again.
What it reads is what the members hold; a block overwritten after a
destroy is gone, and the image says so with the block zeroed and
counted rather than with a guess.
